& [AssessmentIdentity] is exact
  + [assessment_uuid] is 18bd3991-f629-448b-8d21-5831ae5c24d0
  + [conversation_uuid] is cab190f5-eda0-44e0-81a5-51f9605e0baa
  + [baseline_commit] is 0198e11cecbbc53970ee3556c4046238f1f6495b
  + [status] is coherent_language_purpose_revision_candidate_full_SJS_required

& [CoreConclusion] is multi-target semantic programming rather than AI-only compilation
  + existing SOP authority already defines semantic units, meaning contracts, environments, state, instructions, programs, transitions, effects, evaluation, reflection, and one normalized semantic IR
  + existing Cantor authority already defines exact execution, signed packages, query, transition graphs, neural projections, faculty programs, model interfaces, and hardware candidates
  + the new realization supplies one unifying compiler purpose across those formed parts
  + AI generation is one first-class target whose topology and initial state can be compiled from the same articulated program
  + the exact executable fabric remains independently runnable, inspectable, and authoritative when a model is absent
  + the hybrid target lets learned components propose, route, denoise, or articulate while exact contracts validate commitment and effects
  = candidate_purpose: SOP articulates intended meaning as executable semantic programs that compile into proof-carrying exact, neural, and hybrid fabrics
  = current_truth: the architecture is substantially formed but complete language, compiler, conformance, and neural-generation proofs remain unfinished

& [LanguageCompilerRuntimeIdentity] separates three identities
  + [SOP] is the source and semantic programming language
  + [CantorCompiler] preserves, parses, resolves, validates, lowers, optimizes, projects, builds, signs, and packages SOP programs
  + [CantorRuntime] loads admitted packages, evaluates exact instructions, invokes optional models and tools, gates effects, emits traces, and preserves reentry
  = must: let alternative conforming compilers and runtimes implement SOP
  - never: define SOP as only the current Rust implementation, Cantor database, Codex workflow, or one neural architecture

& [CompilationSpine] is one meaning-preserving path
  * preserve exact source and dependencies
  * parse supported surface forms without interpreting unsupported syntax
  * resolve names, contexts, imports, identities, and versions
  * type and relation check terms, roles, contracts, states, and transitions
  * validate inference, authority, constraints, effects, faults, and termination
  * lower verbose, condensed, and machine forms to one canonical SemanticIR
  * optimize only through behavior-preserving transformations with proof obligations
  * select one or more target profiles
  * build target artifacts and correspondence manifests
  * test equivalence, conformance, determinism, safety, fallback, and declared approximation
  * sign and admit source, IR, targets, proofs, and toolchain identities as one compatible package family
  * execute, observe, review, reenter, and optionally learn without rewriting source truth
  = must: expose semantic loss, approximation, provider dependence, and nondeterminism at every target boundary

& [ExactExecutableFabricTarget] is SOP-X
  + [representation] is typed SemanticIR, instruction graph, transition graph, contract automata, indexes, state schema, effect gates, and trace forms
  + [execution] is deterministic where inputs and observations are deterministic
  + [capability] is declare, scope, relate, attend, infer, constrain, transform, control, effect, and emit through typed operations
  + [authority] is canonical runtime behavior for admitted supported programs
  + [fallback] remains available whenever optional learned targets are absent, stale, uncertain, or rejected
  = must: make every state change and effect path inspectable before commitment

& [NeuralFabricTarget] is SOP-N
  + [representation] is one validated NeuralFabricBlueprint plus deterministic and learned parameter classes
  + [shape] may mirror semantic identities, relations, boundaries, purposes, faculty programs, and transition neighborhoods under an explicit correspondence manifest
  + [execution] proposes retrieval, routing, ambiguity resolution, transition choice, fault prediction, or articulation
  + [construction] may use deterministic semantic initialization, LLM-proposed priors, training, distillation, hypernetworks, or weight generation
  + [authority] remains advisory unless a separately signed target profile proves a narrower deterministic property
  = must: resolve every semantically material output to exact identities, declared auxiliary signals, or abstention
  - never: store authoritative meaning, permission, or source truth only in learned parameters

& [HybridExecutableFabricTarget] is SOP-H
  + [exact_plane] owns identities, contracts, state, authority, hard constraints, effects, proof, rollback, and termination
  + [learned_plane] owns bounded proposals, rankings, estimates, generated candidates, and provider articulation
  + [junction] projects the smallest necessary semantic state into a learned component and receives typed candidate output
  + [gate] admits, qualifies, remasks, challenges, branches, rejects, or abstains before any state or effect commitment
  + [fallback] continues through SOP-X or fails visibly when the learned plane is unavailable
  = must: preserve one replayable account across both planes

& [ProjectionTargets] are derived interfaces rather than rival language identities
  + [SOP-A] is attention and inference frames projected into model-visible context
  + [SOP-D] is relational, graph, document, temporal, provenance, vector, and query storage
  + [SOP-T] is typed model, tool, agent, service, and cooperative-junction interfaces
  + [SOP-W] is workflow, faculty, planner, and Weaver execution programs
  + [SOP-F] is FPGA bytecode, fixed-function accelerators, or native hardware profiles
  + [SOP-I] is human-readable explanation, source maps, diagnostics, and interactive inspection
  = must: derive every projection from the same admitted semantic identities and package lineage

& [SimpleContractEntry] explains portability without hiding complexity
  + [entry_contract] names purpose, inputs, preconditions, invariants, authority, effects, outputs, faults, and termination
  + [recursive_loading] resolves concise referenced terms and operations into their signed meaning contracts as needed
  + [minimum_frame] loads only materially pertinent definitions, dependencies, and target capabilities
  + [execution] follows the compiled program rather than asking a provider to improvise missing semantics
  = must: permit a simple surface contract to address a deeply compiled fabric
  - never: call a contract simple when its governing dependencies, permissions, or failure paths are implicit

& [InstructionExecutionBoundary] prevents neural proposal from becoming action by wording alone
  + [declaration] changes the semantic environment only through admitted program semantics
  + [pure_instruction] transforms inspectable semantic state without external effects
  + [inference_instruction] produces a judgment under one declared regime and preserves premises and uncertainty
  + [neural_instruction] invokes a model target and returns a typed provider-dependent candidate
  + [effect_instruction] requires explicit capability, authority, validation, commitment, observation, and compensation behavior
  + [meta_instruction] may inspect or compile SOP but cannot self-expand its authority
  = must: distinguish describing an instruction, compiling it, proposing it, evaluating it, authorizing it, and committing its effects

& [NeuralPrinterDelineation] identifies the actual compiler capability
  + [print_blueprint] emits inspectable topology, modules, masks, initialization classes, output mappings, and correspondence
  + [print_initial_state] deterministically materializes valid candidate tensors from one blueprint and target profile
  + [print_calibrated_model] trains or calibrates under a frozen corpus and evaluation contract
  + [print_generated_weights] uses a separately trained weight generator to synthesize parameter blocks
  + [print_runtime_package] binds model, tokenizer or vocabulary, target profile, semantic fabric, corpus, metrics, proof, and provider format
  = must: state which print stage has been achieved
  = must: keep a failed or weak printed model from weakening the exact fabric

& [ProgrammingLanguageCompletenessGap] is still material
  + [substantially_formed] is language identity, semantic data model, instruction families, contracts, programs, transitions, evaluation cycle, reflection, trust, and bounded parser and runtime implementations
  + [incomplete] is stable grammar, general parser, static semantics, type and effect checker, module system, optimizer laws, compiler target interface, debugger, diagnostics, standard library boundary, conformance suite, and compatibility policy
  + [neural_incomplete] is target profile, blueprint schema, tensor builder, reviewed corpus, training pipeline, held-out evaluation, admission, and provider packaging
  + [ecosystem_incomplete] is independent implementation proof, package registry, version negotiation, migration tooling, and long-term governance
  = conclusion: SOP is a formed semantic programming language architecture with a working bounded kernel, not yet a fully realized general-purpose language implementation

& [PurposeRevisionCompatibility] protects the existing SOP body
  + [compatible] existing declarations, ontologies, attention directives, faculty programs, database forms, model profiles, and narrative records remain source or libraries under the wider compiler purpose
  + [data_instruction_distinction] descriptive facts and source quotations do not become executable merely because they are valid SOP
  + [profile_distinction] a document declares whether it is data, definition, contract, program, test, proof, policy, narrative, or mixed under explicit boundaries
  + [legacy_surface] unsupported historic forms remain preserved and require an explicit lowering profile before execution
  + [migration] records original interpretation, target interpretation, losses, new obligations, and rollback
  = must: prove that the purpose revision adds compiler organization without silently changing existing program meaning or authority

& [MetaCircularPotential] is powerful but bootstrap-bounded
  + [self_description] SOP may articulate its grammar, semantic forms, compiler passes, target profiles, tests, and runtime contracts
  + [self_hosting] Cantor may compile an admitted SOP description of bounded parts of its own compiler
  + [bootstrap_root] one independently trusted implementation, toolchain, signature policy, and conformance fixture remains necessary
  + [reflection] can inspect and improve candidate compiler behavior without self-signing correctness
  - never: infer that self-description proves faithful implementation or grants self-modification authority

& [FacultyCompilerRoles] map the existing faculties into compilation
  + [Observer] opens the job, records state, coordinates returns, and disposes the final target package
  + [Honesty] checks identity, source correspondence, semantic equivalence, uncertainty, and claims
  + [Security] checks authority, capability, boundaries, effects, supply chain, isolation, and admission
  + [Scribe] preserves source, attribution, normalized inscriptions, source maps, and diagnostics
  + [Refiner] minimizes and clarifies IR, contracts, transformations, and target expression without semantic loss
  + [Weaver] forms compatible identity, operation, module, backend, and dependency compositions
  + [Planner] orders passes, proofs, builds, deployments, effects, fallback, and recovery
  = must: compile faculty programs themselves through the same language contracts rather than grant them implicit behavior

& [FirstEndToEndProof] is one tiny multi-target semantic program
  + [program] declares two contextual identities, one operation, one constraint, one inferred judgment, one denied effect, one emitted result, and one explicit unresolved alternative
  + [source_forms] are one verbose and one condensed form expected to lower to equivalent IR
  + [SOP_X] executes the exact program and emits byte-stable normalized state and trace
  + [SOP_N] proposes identity and operation routes from a compiled blueprint without effect authority
  + [SOP_H] imports the proposal, rejects one forbidden route, executes the valid exact transition, and emits the same admitted result plus provider lineage
  + [faults] include malformed source, ambiguous binding, unsigned dependency, stale model, forbidden effect, unsupported target feature, and neural abstention
  + [replay] reconstructs source, IR, targets, decisions, output, and residual uncertainty
  + [baseline] is the current deterministic core without a learned target

& [AcceptanceDimensions] measure whether the unifying purpose is real
  + [semantic_preservation] is equivalent source forms and target projections retain intended meaning
  + [execution_correctness] is transitions, constraints, effects, faults, and termination match the exact semantics
  + [cross_target_correspondence] is every neural or interface element maps to exact identities or declared loss
  + [proof_completeness] is source, compiler, IR, target, runtime, provider, effect, and result lineage
  + [fallback_correctness] is exact behavior remains valid without learned targets
  + [conformance] is another implementation can reproduce the normalized fixture behavior
  + [work] is source size, IR size, build time, runtime time, memory, model size, tokens, and review effort

& [FaultSet] is mandatory
  + [AI_reduction] defines SOP only by model generation and discards exact semantics
  + [language_completion_claim] calls the architecture fully realized before grammar, conformance, and implementation proofs
  + [description_execution_confusion] treats every valid statement as an authorized instruction
  + [backend_semantic_drift] lets each target acquire incompatible meanings for one identity
  + [optimizer_semantic_loss] shortens or reorganizes a program without equivalence proof
  + [learned_authority] lets model confidence, weights, or behavior determine permission
  + [simple_contract_ellipsis] hides material dependencies or effects behind a concise entry contract
  + [self_hosting_circularity] lets compiler self-description certify its own correctness
  + [target_proliferation] adds backends without stable target interface or measured requirement
  + [migration_reinterpretation] changes historical SOP meaning through the new purpose label

& [CandidateRequirementSet] is SMTC-001 through SMTC-030
  + [SMTC-001] define SOP as an inspectable semantic programming language with multi-target compilation
  + [SMTC-002] adopt the candidate purpose of proof-carrying exact, neural, and hybrid fabrics
  + [SMTC-003] keep SOP language, Cantor compiler, and Cantor runtime identities separable
  + [SMTC-004] preserve one canonical SemanticIR across supported surface forms and targets
  + [SMTC-005] define a staged source-to-package compilation spine
  + [SMTC-006] make SOP-X independently executable and authoritative for supported deterministic semantics
  + [SMTC-007] make SOP-N a fabric-bound neural proposal and generation target
  + [SMTC-008] make SOP-H an exact-governed learned execution target with fallback
  + [SMTC-009] define attention, data, tool, workflow, hardware, and inspection projections as derived targets
  + [SMTC-010] bind all targets to stable semantic identities and package lineage
  + [SMTC-011] let a concise entry contract recursively address complete signed meaning
  + [SMTC-012] distinguish description, compilation, proposal, evaluation, authorization, and effect commitment
  + [SMTC-013] distinguish blueprint, initial tensor, calibrated model, generated weight, and runtime-package printing
  + [SMTC-014] prohibit authoritative meaning and permission from residing only in neural weights
  + [SMTC-015] define document roles so valid SOP data does not become executable by default
  + [SMTC-016] preserve unsupported legacy forms until explicit lowering and migration authority exists
  + [SMTC-017] require semantic equivalence proof for compiler optimization
  + [SMTC-018] define grammar, static semantics, types, effects, modules, diagnostics, and standard library boundaries
  + [SMTC-019] define stable compiler-target and runtime interfaces
  + [SMTC-020] build a cross-implementation conformance suite and compatibility policy
  + [SMTC-021] bind neural blueprint, corpus, builder, training, evaluation, and provider artifacts
  + [SMTC-022] preserve exact fallback under absent, stale, uncertain, rejected, or failed neural targets
  + [SMTC-023] compile faculty programs through explicit SOP contracts
  + [SMTC-024] preserve a non-circular trusted bootstrap for self-description and self-hosting
  + [SMTC-025] prove compatibility and reversible migration before revising canonical SOP purpose
  + [SMTC-026] implement one tiny exact, neural, and hybrid multi-target proof before wider language claims
  + [SMTC-027] measure semantic preservation, execution, correspondence, proof, fallback, conformance, and work
  + [SMTC-028] fail visibly on unsupported target features and semantic loss
  + [SMTC-029] require signed requirements and measured benefit before adding target profiles
  + [SMTC-030] retain all current canonical authority until the full purpose revision passes SJS

& [Decision] is preserve the purpose revision and make full SJS the next language-identity gate
  + [fit] is high because the candidate organizes rather than contradicts current SOP and Cantor architecture
  + [canonical_authority] SOP Core, Semantic Programming Identity, virtual machine, neural boundary, and current signed profiles remain authoritative
  + [new_canonical_authority] is absent
  + [new_implementation_authority] is absent
  + [purpose_revision_authority] is absent
  + [schedule_effect] add SMTC-SJS-01 as the umbrella language-purpose reconciliation before treating all SOP as compiler input by default
  + [first_proof_after_authority] is one tiny multi-target exact, blueprint, and hybrid program over existing bounded core forms
  + [reasoning_depth] language-purpose reconciliation, compatibility, target semantics, and bootstrap require deeper architecture reasoning; closed fixture implementation becomes Spark-suitable only after signed activation authority
